package teyru

/* Gson's tree API (com.google.gson), so a Java file that walks a JSON document
   with JsonParser/JsonObject/JsonArray ports over unchanged.

   The shapes and the observable behaviour follow Gson 2.10.0, including the
   details that are easy to get wrong:

   - JsonNull extends JsonElement, not JsonPrimitive (Gson changed this in 2.10),
     so isJsonPrimitive() is false for it and getAsString() throws.
   - A parsed number keeps the literal it was written with. The parser stores a
     JsonLazyNumber holding the source text, so "1e5" comes back out as 1e5 and
     "1.00" as 1.00 -- never as 100000.0 or 1.0.
   - Escaping follows Gson's JsonWriter replacement table exactly: " and \ are
     escaped, the five short forms \b \t \n \f \r are used, every other byte
     below 0x20 becomes \u00xx with lower-case hex, / is NOT escaped, non-ASCII
     is emitted as UTF-8 rather than as \u escapes (only U+2028 and U+2029 are
     escaped), and the HTML-sensitive characters < > & = ' are escaped only when
     the writer is htmlSafe. That last flag is the whole difference between
     JsonElement.toString() (htmlSafe false) and a default Gson.toJson tree
     write (htmlSafe true), and both directions are exercised by t93_json.
   - Pretty printing indents by two spaces, writes ": " after a name, and keeps
     {} and [] on one line -- empty containers are the case most writers get
     wrong.

   Deviations from Gson, all deliberate and all visible in tests:

   - JsonParser.parseString is strict (RFC 8259). Gson parses in lenient mode,
     so it accepts {a:1}, 'single quotes', 01, +1, .5, 1., NaN, [1,] and a bare
     "-"; this parser rejects all of them with JsonSyntaxException, rejects an
     empty document instead of returning JsonNull, and always rejects trailing
     data (Gson skips that check when the document is exactly `null`).
   - JsonObject.entrySet() returns a List<JsonMember> and keySet() a
     List<String>: the standard library has no Set and Map declares no nested
     Entry, so the two names Gson returns collection types for return the
     ordered lists this library does have. JsonMember.getKey/getValue mirror
     Map.Entry.
   - fromJson(String, Class) is not here. `JsonObject.class` has type Object in
     this language and a generic method whose body casts to its own type
     variable does not compile at the C level, so Gson's signature could be
     written but never called; fromJson(String) returning JsonElement is the
     usable form of the same thing.
   - Gson's reflective binding (fromJson into a user class) is not this
     class's: the compiler generates the reader for a class it can see. Numbers
     beyond long, getAsBigDecimal, getAsBigInteger and
     the lenient reader are absent for the same kind of reason; each is called
     out where it would have lived.

   One compiler quirk is worked around rather than left to the caller: a
   primitive argument does not select the overload it should. With four
   constructors taking Boolean, Number, String and Character, `new
   JsonPrimitive('x')` runs the Boolean one (the first declared) with a
   Character as its argument, and `new JsonPrimitive(5)` does too. The
   constructors therefore store their argument by its runtime class -- see
   stored() below -- so the value held is the one Gson's constructor would have
   held even though the parameter's declared type is wrong. A callback to a
   user's own class with overloads like these needs the same treatment. */

/* com.google.gson.JsonParseException. NumberFormatException does not exist in
   this standard library, so the numeric accessors below throw
   IllegalArgumentException where Gson throws NumberFormatException: a handler
   that catches IllegalArgumentException (NumberFormatException's parent) keeps
   working, one that names NumberFormatException does not compile. */
class JsonParseException extends RuntimeException {
  public JsonParseException() {
  }

  public JsonParseException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }

  public JsonParseException(Throwable cause) {
    super(cause)
  }
}

class JsonSyntaxException extends JsonParseException {
  public JsonSyntaxException() {
  }

  public JsonSyntaxException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }

  public JsonSyntaxException(Throwable cause) {
    super(cause)
  }
}

/* com.google.gson.JsonElement. The four isJson* predicates are plain
   instanceof, as in Gson, and the four getAsJson* narrowings throw
   IllegalStateException carrying the rendered element, which is Gson's
   message format ("Not a JSON Object: {...}") -- it is why toString has to
   work on every element, including a partially built one.

   Every accessor whose body always throws ends with an unreachable return.
   Java accepts a body that cannot complete normally (JLS 14.21), but this
   checker only accepts a literal trailing return statement: a method ending in
   `throw` is reported as TY-TYP-0020 "missing return statement". The dead
   statement is what makes the body legal; the throw above it is what runs. */
abstract class JsonElement {
  public abstract JsonElement deepCopy()

  public boolean isJsonObject() {
    return this instanceof JsonObject
  }

  public boolean isJsonArray() {
    return this instanceof JsonArray
  }

  public boolean isJsonPrimitive() {
    return this instanceof JsonPrimitive
  }

  public boolean isJsonNull() {
    return this instanceof JsonNull
  }

  public JsonObject getAsJsonObject() {
    if (isJsonObject()) {
      return (JsonObject) this
    }
    throw new IllegalStateException("Not a JSON Object: " + this)
    return null
  }

  public JsonArray getAsJsonArray() {
    if (isJsonArray()) {
      return (JsonArray) this
    }
    throw new IllegalStateException("Not a JSON Array: " + this)
    return null
  }

  public JsonPrimitive getAsJsonPrimitive() {
    if (isJsonPrimitive()) {
      return (JsonPrimitive) this
    }
    throw new IllegalStateException("Not a JSON Primitive: " + this)
    return null
  }

  public JsonNull getAsJsonNull() {
    if (isJsonNull()) {
      return (JsonNull) this
    }
    throw new IllegalStateException("Not a JSON Null: " + this)
    return null
  }

  // Gson puts the class's simple name in these UnsupportedOperationException
  // messages. Class exposes only getName() here, and that is qualified
  // ("teyru.JsonObject"), so the last dot is cut by hand to keep the message
  // byte-for-byte what Java would print.
  public boolean getAsBoolean() {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(simpleName(this))
    return false
  }

  public Number getAsNumber() {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(simpleName(this))
    return null
  }

  public String getAsString() {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(simpleName(this))
    return null
  }

  public double getAsDouble() {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(simpleName(this))
    return 0.0
  }

  public float getAsFloat() {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(simpleName(this))
    return 0.0f
  }

  public long getAsLong() {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(simpleName(this))
    return 0L
  }

  public int getAsInt() {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(simpleName(this))
    return 0
  }

  public short getAsShort() {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(simpleName(this))
    return (short) 0
  }

  public byte getAsByte() {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(simpleName(this))
    return (byte) 0
  }

  public char getAsCharacter() {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(simpleName(this))
    return (char) 0
  }

  /* The compact rendering, which is what every element prints as. Gson builds
     it with a fresh JsonWriter whose indent is null and whose htmlSafe is
     false, and so serializeNulls stays at the writer's own default of true --
     which is why {"a":null} survives toString() but disappears from
     new Gson().toJson(...), whose serializeNulls is false. */
  public String toString() {
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder()
    JsonWriter writer = new JsonWriter(sb)
    JsonWriter.write(this, writer)
    return sb.toString()
  }

  private static String simpleName(Object o) {
    String name = o.getClass().getName()
    int dot = -1
    for (int i = 0 : i < name.length() : i++) {
      if (name.charAt(i) == '.') {
        dot = i
      }
    }
    if (dot < 0) {
      return name
    }
    return name.substring(dot + 1, name.length())
  }
}

/* com.google.gson.JsonObject. Members keep insertion order, as they do in
   Gson's LinkedTreeMap, and re-adding an existing name replaces the value
   without moving the member: {"a":1,"b":2} plus a=3 prints a first. */
class JsonObject extends JsonElement {
  private JsonMembers members

  public JsonObject() {
    members = new JsonMembers()
  }

  public JsonElement deepCopy() {
    JsonObject copy = new JsonObject()
    for (int i = 0 : i < members.size() : i++) {
      copy.members.put(members.keyAt(i), members.valueAt(i).deepCopy())
    }
    return copy
  }

  // A null value is stored as JsonNull.INSTANCE, never as a null member: a
  // JsonObject cannot hold a Java null, which is what makes the null-friendlier
  // readers trip over it.
  public void add(String property, JsonElement value) {
    members.put(property, JsonNull.orNull(value))
  }

  public void addProperty(String property, String value) {
    if (value == null) {
      add(property, null)
      return
    }
    add(property, new JsonPrimitive(value))
  }

  public void addProperty(String property, Number value) {
    if (value == null) {
      add(property, null)
      return
    }
    add(property, new JsonPrimitive(value))
  }

  public void addProperty(String property, Boolean value) {
    if (value == null) {
      add(property, null)
      return
    }
    add(property, new JsonPrimitive(value))
  }

  public void addProperty(String property, Character value) {
    if (value == null) {
      add(property, null)
      return
    }
    add(property, new JsonPrimitive(value))
  }

  public JsonElement remove(String property) {
    return members.remove(property)
  }

  public List<JsonMember> entrySet() {
    ArrayList<JsonMember> out = new ArrayList<JsonMember>(members.size() > 0 ? members.size() : 1)
    for (int i = 0 : i < members.size() : i++) {
      out.add(new JsonMember(members.keyAt(i), members.valueAt(i)))
    }
    return out
  }

  public List<String> keySet() {
    ArrayList<String> out = new ArrayList<String>(members.size() > 0 ? members.size() : 1)
    for (int i = 0 : i < members.size() : i++) {
      out.add(members.keyAt(i))
    }
    return out
  }

  public int size() {
    return members.size()
  }

  public boolean isEmpty() {
    return members.isEmpty()
  }

  public boolean has(String memberName) {
    return members.has(memberName)
  }

  public JsonElement get(String memberName) {
    return members.get(memberName)
  }

  public JsonPrimitive getAsJsonPrimitive(String memberName) {
    return (JsonPrimitive) members.get(memberName)
  }

  public JsonArray getAsJsonArray(String memberName) {
    return (JsonArray) members.get(memberName)
  }

  public JsonObject getAsJsonObject(String memberName) {
    return (JsonObject) members.get(memberName)
  }

  // Gson compares the backing maps, so member order is not part of equality.
  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (o == this) {
      return true
    }
    if (!(o instanceof JsonObject)) {
      return false
    }
    return members.sameAs(((JsonObject) o).members)
  }

  public int hashCode() {
    return members.hash()
  }
}

/* One member of a JsonObject, standing in for Java's Map.Entry<String,
   JsonElement> (see the header). */
class JsonMember {
  private String key
  private JsonElement value

  public JsonMember(String key, JsonElement value) {
    this.key = key
    this.value = value
  }

  public String getKey() {
    return key
  }

  public JsonElement getValue() {
    return value
  }

  public String toString() {
    return key + "=" + value
  }
}

/* The ordered name -> element map behind JsonObject. Gson's LinkedTreeMap is a
   hash map over a doubly linked list; this is the same idea in the shape the
   rest of the library uses (parallel arrays plus an open-addressed index), with
   the entries kept dense so that printing walks them in insertion order. */
class JsonMembers {
  private String[] keys
  private JsonElement[] values
  private int count
  private int[] index
  private int mask

  public JsonMembers() {
    keys = new String[8]
    values = new JsonElement[8]
    index = new int[16]
    mask = 15
    clearIndex()
  }

  public int size() {
    return count
  }

  public boolean isEmpty() {
    return count == 0
  }

  public String keyAt(int i) {
    return keys[i]
  }

  public JsonElement valueAt(int i) {
    return values[i]
  }

  public boolean has(String key) {
    return find(key) >= 0
  }

  public JsonElement get(String key) {
    int slot = find(key)
    if (slot < 0) {
      return null
    }
    return values[slot]
  }

  public void put(String key, JsonElement value) {
    int slot = find(key)
    if (slot >= 0) {
      values[slot] = value
      return
    }
    if (count == keys.length) {
      grow()
    }
    if ((count + 1) * 4 >= index.length * 3) {
      reindex(index.length * 2)
    }
    keys[count] = key
    values[count] = value
    place(key, count)
    count++
  }

  public JsonElement remove(String key) {
    int slot = find(key)
    if (slot < 0) {
      return null
    }
    JsonElement old = values[slot]
    for (int i = slot : i < count - 1 : i++) {
      keys[i] = keys[i + 1]
      values[i] = values[i + 1]
    }
    count--
    keys[count] = null
    values[count] = null
    // every position after the hole moved, so the index is rebuilt rather than
    // patched: removal is rare and this keeps one code path correct
    clearIndex()
    for (int i = 0 : i < count : i++) {
      place(keys[i], i)
    }
    return old
  }

  // JsonObject.equals ignores order; so does this.
  public boolean sameAs(JsonMembers other) {
    if (other.size() != count) {
      return false
    }
    for (int i = 0 : i < count : i++) {
      if (!other.has(keys[i])) {
        return false
      }
      if (!values[i].equals(other.get(keys[i]))) {
        return false
      }
    }
    return true
  }

  /* An order-insensitive hash to match sameAs. The accumulation is done in a
     long against a prime modulus: combining 32-bit hashes in int arithmetic
     would overflow, and signed overflow is not defined by the C the compiler
     emits, so the hashes are kept inside a range where no multiplication can
     leave it. The numbers themselves are not Java's -- only the contract is. */
  public int hash() {
    long h = 0
    for (int i = 0 : i < count : i++) {
      long member = ((long) keys[i].hashCode()) * 31L + (long) values[i].hashCode()
      h = (h + member) % 1000000007L
    }
    return (int) h
  }

  public String toString() {
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder()
    sb.append("{")
    for (int i = 0 : i < count : i++) {
      if (i > 0) {
        sb.append(", ")
      }
      sb.append(keys[i]).append("=").append(values[i])
    }
    sb.append("}")
    return sb.toString()
  }

  private int find(String key) {
    if (key == null) {
      // Gson's LinkedTreeMap fails on a null key too, but with a bare
      // NullPointerException from inside compareTo; say what went wrong
      throw new NullPointerException("null property name")
    }
    int probe = slotOf(key)
    for (int i = 0 : i < index.length : i++) {
      int position = index[probe]
      if (position < 0) {
        return -1
      }
      if (key.equals(keys[position])) {
        return position
      }
      probe = (probe + 1) & mask
    }
    return -1
  }

  private int slotOf(String key) {
    return (key.hashCode() & 0x7fffffff) & mask
  }

  // The index stores the position of the member at each slot, so a second name
  // that hashes to an occupied slot has to take the next free one: writing over
  // the slot would lose the member already there.
  private void place(String key, int position) {
    int probe = slotOf(key)
    while (index[probe] >= 0) {
      probe = (probe + 1) & mask
    }
    index[probe] = position
  }

  private void clearIndex() {
    for (int i = 0 : i < index.length : i++) {
      index[i] = -1
    }
  }

  private void grow() {
    String[] biggerKeys = new String[keys.length * 2]
    JsonElement[] biggerValues = new JsonElement[values.length * 2]
    for (int i = 0 : i < count : i++) {
      biggerKeys[i] = keys[i]
      biggerValues[i] = values[i]
    }
    keys = biggerKeys
    values = biggerValues
  }

  private void reindex(int size) {
    index = new int[size]
    mask = size - 1
    clearIndex()
    for (int i = 0 : i < count : i++) {
      place(keys[i], i)
    }
  }
}

/* com.google.gson.JsonArray, including Iterable<JsonElement> so that
   `for (JsonElement e : array)` works the way it does in Java. */
class JsonArray extends JsonElement implements Iterable<JsonElement> {
  private ArrayList<JsonElement> elements

  public JsonArray() {
    elements = new ArrayList<JsonElement>()
  }

  public JsonArray(int capacity) {
    elements = new ArrayList<JsonElement>(capacity)
  }

  public JsonElement deepCopy() {
    JsonArray copy = new JsonArray(elements.size())
    for (int i = 0 : i < elements.size() : i++) {
      copy.elements.add(elements.get(i).deepCopy())
    }
    return copy
  }

  public void add(Boolean bool) {
    if (bool == null) {
      elements.add(JsonNull.INSTANCE)
      return
    }
    elements.add(new JsonPrimitive(bool))
  }

  public void add(Character character) {
    if (character == null) {
      elements.add(JsonNull.INSTANCE)
      return
    }
    elements.add(new JsonPrimitive(character))
  }

  public void add(Number number) {
    if (number == null) {
      elements.add(JsonNull.INSTANCE)
      return
    }
    elements.add(new JsonPrimitive(number))
  }

  public void add(String string) {
    if (string == null) {
      elements.add(JsonNull.INSTANCE)
      return
    }
    elements.add(new JsonPrimitive(string))
  }

  public void add(JsonElement element) {
    elements.add(JsonNull.orNull(element))
  }

  public void addAll(JsonArray array) {
    for (int i = 0 : i < array.size() : i++) {
      elements.add(array.get(i))
    }
  }

  public JsonElement set(int index, JsonElement element) {
    return elements.set(index, JsonNull.orNull(element))
  }

  public boolean remove(JsonElement element) {
    return elements.remove(element)
  }

  public JsonElement remove(int index) {
    return elements.removeAt(index)
  }

  public boolean contains(JsonElement element) {
    return elements.contains(element)
  }

  public int size() {
    return elements.size()
  }

  public boolean isEmpty() {
    return elements.isEmpty()
  }

  public Iterator<JsonElement> iterator() {
    return elements.iterator()
  }

  public JsonElement get(int i) {
    return elements.get(i)
  }

  /* Gson lets an array stand in for its single element in every getAs*
     conversion and refuses loudly otherwise, which is what tells a caller that
     [1,2] is not the number 1. */
  private JsonElement single() {
    if (elements.size() == 1) {
      return elements.get(0)
    }
    throw new IllegalStateException("Array must have size 1, but has size " + elements.size())
    return null
  }

  public Number getAsNumber() {
    return single().getAsNumber()
  }

  public String getAsString() {
    return single().getAsString()
  }

  public boolean getAsBoolean() {
    return single().getAsBoolean()
  }

  public double getAsDouble() {
    return single().getAsDouble()
  }

  public float getAsFloat() {
    return single().getAsFloat()
  }

  public long getAsLong() {
    return single().getAsLong()
  }

  public int getAsInt() {
    return single().getAsInt()
  }

  public short getAsShort() {
    return single().getAsShort()
  }

  public byte getAsByte() {
    return single().getAsByte()
  }

  public char getAsCharacter() {
    return single().getAsCharacter()
  }

  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (o == this) {
      return true
    }
    if (!(o instanceof JsonArray)) {
      return false
    }
    JsonArray other = (JsonArray) o
    if (other.size() != size()) {
      return false
    }
    for (int i = 0 : i < size() : i++) {
      if (!elements.get(i).equals(other.get(i))) {
        return false
      }
    }
    return true
  }

  public int hashCode() {
    long h = 1
    for (int i = 0 : i < size() : i++) {
      h = (h * 31L + (long) elements.get(i).hashCode()) % 1000000007L
    }
    return (int) h
  }
}

/* com.google.gson.JsonPrimitive. The value is one of Boolean, Number or
   String; a char is turned into a one-character String by the constructor, as
   in Gson, because JSON has no character type. */
class JsonPrimitive extends JsonElement {
  private Object value

  public JsonPrimitive(Boolean bool) {
    value = stored(bool)
  }

  public JsonPrimitive(Number number) {
    value = stored(number)
  }

  public JsonPrimitive(String string) {
    value = stored(string)
  }

  public JsonPrimitive(Character c) {
    value = stored(c)
  }

  /* The argument that arrives here is not always the type this constructor
     declares. A primitive at the call site does not select the constructor it
     should: `new JsonPrimitive('x')` reaches the Boolean one with a Character
     in hand, because the checker falls back to the first declared overload when
     no parameter matches the argument exactly (see the file header). Storing by
     the runtime class instead of trusting the parameter is what keeps every
     construction path holding what Gson would hold, and a char is still turned
     into the one-character string JSON wants. The null is checked here, as
     Gson's checkNotNull does, so a null argument throws whichever overload the
     call site landed on. */
  private static Object stored(Object v) {
    if (v == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException()
    }
    if (v instanceof Character) {
      return String.valueOf((Character) v)
    }
    return v
  }

  public JsonElement deepCopy() {
    return this
  }

  public boolean isBoolean() {
    return value instanceof Boolean
  }

  public boolean isNumber() {
    return value instanceof Number
  }

  public boolean isString() {
    return value instanceof String
  }

  public boolean getAsBoolean() {
    if (isBoolean()) {
      return (Boolean) value
    }
    return JsonNumbers.parseBoolean(getAsString())
  }

  /* A string primitive answers with a lazily parsed number, exactly as Gson
     does, which is what keeps "5e3" printing as 5e3 while getAsInt() still
     reads it as 5000. */
  public Number getAsNumber() {
    if (isNumber()) {
      return (Number) value
    }
    if (isString()) {
      return new JsonLazyNumber((String) value)
    }
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Primitive is neither a number nor a string")
    return null
  }

  public String getAsString() {
    if (isString()) {
      return (String) value
    }
    if (isNumber()) {
      return getAsNumber().toString()
    }
    if (isBoolean()) {
      return ((Boolean) value).toString()
    }
    throw new AssertionError("Unexpected value type")
    return null
  }

  public double getAsDouble() {
    return isNumber() ? getAsNumber().doubleValue() : JsonNumbers.parseDouble(getAsString())
  }

  public float getAsFloat() {
    return isNumber() ? getAsNumber().floatValue() : (float) JsonNumbers.parseDouble(getAsString())
  }

  public long getAsLong() {
    return isNumber() ? getAsNumber().longValue() : JsonNumbers.parseLong(getAsString())
  }

  public int getAsInt() {
    return isNumber() ? getAsNumber().intValue() : JsonNumbers.parseInt(getAsString())
  }

  public short getAsShort() {
    return isNumber() ? getAsNumber().shortValue() : (short) JsonNumbers.parseInt(getAsString())
  }

  public byte getAsByte() {
    return isNumber() ? getAsNumber().byteValue() : (byte) JsonNumbers.parseInt(getAsString())
  }

  public char getAsCharacter() {
    String s = getAsString()
    if (s.length() == 0) {
      throw new UnsupportedOperationException("String value is empty")
    }
    // the first byte of a UTF-8 string, where Java takes the first UTF-16 code
    // unit: the language's String is bytes and cannot offer the other
    return (char) s.charAt(0)
  }

  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (o == this) {
      return true
    }
    if (o == null || !(o instanceof JsonPrimitive)) {
      return false
    }
    JsonPrimitive other = (JsonPrimitive) o
    if (isIntegral(this) && isIntegral(other)) {
      return getAsNumber().longValue() == other.getAsNumber().longValue()
    }
    if (isNumber() && other.isNumber()) {
      double a = getAsNumber().doubleValue()
      double b = other.getAsNumber().doubleValue()
      return a == b
    }
    /* Gson ends with `value.equals(other.value)`, which cannot be trusted here:
       the runtime's Integer.equals coerces its argument, so Integer(1) answers
       true for the string "1" and a number would compare equal to a string.
       Only a string against a string or a boolean against a boolean can reach
       this point -- every pair of numbers was settled above -- so asking for the
       same kind refuses the coerced comparison and otherwise agrees with Gson. */
    if (isString() && other.isString()) {
      return value.equals(other.value)
    }
    if (isBoolean() && other.isBoolean()) {
      return value.equals(other.value)
    }
    return false
  }

  /* Gson's own hash: integral numbers hash by their long value and other
     numbers by their double bits, so 1 and 1.0 -- which its equals() calls
     equal -- hash differently. That quirk is kept rather than fixed, because a
     caller may be relying on either half of it. */
  public int hashCode() {
    if (value == null) {
      return 31
    }
    if (isIntegral(this)) {
      long v = getAsNumber().longValue()
      return (int) (v ^ (v >>> 32))
    }
    if (isNumber()) {
      return Double.valueOf(getAsNumber().doubleValue()).hashCode()
    }
    return value.hashCode()
  }

  private static boolean isIntegral(JsonPrimitive primitive) {
    return primitive.value instanceof Byte || primitive.value instanceof Short ||
      primitive.value instanceof Integer || primitive.value instanceof Long
  }
}

/* com.google.gson.internal.LazilyParsedNumber: the number behind a primitive
   that was read from text. It keeps the text so that a value which never
   leaves the document is printed back unchanged, and parses only when somebody
   asks for a numeric type. */
class JsonLazyNumber extends Number {
  private String text

  public JsonLazyNumber(String text) {
    this.text = text
  }

  /* Gson tries Integer.parseInt, then (int) Long.parseLong, then
     BigDecimal.intValue(). The first two are reproduced exactly; the third is
     not, because there is no arbitrary-precision decimal here -- an integer
     literal past Long.MAX_VALUE saturates instead of wrapping around, which is
     the one input where this differs (see t93_json's report line). */
  public int intValue() {
    if (JsonNumbers.isIntegerText(text)) {
      try {
        return (int) JsonNumbers.parseLong(text)
      } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
        return JsonNumbers.intOf(doubleValue())
      }
    }
    return JsonNumbers.intOf(doubleValue())
  }

  public long longValue() {
    if (JsonNumbers.isIntegerText(text)) {
      try {
        return JsonNumbers.parseLong(text)
      } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
        return JsonNumbers.longOf(doubleValue())
      }
    }
    return JsonNumbers.longOf(doubleValue())
  }

  public double doubleValue() {
    return JsonNumbers.parseDouble(text)
  }

  public float floatValue() {
    return (float) JsonNumbers.parseDouble(text)
  }

  public byte byteValue() {
    return (byte) intValue()
  }

  public short shortValue() {
    return (short) intValue()
  }

  public String toString() {
    return text
  }

  public int hashCode() {
    return text.hashCode()
  }
}

/* com.google.gson.JsonNull. Since 2.10 it is a JsonElement and not a
   JsonPrimitive, so it has no getAs* accessors of its own and every conversion
   on it throws. */
class JsonNull extends JsonElement {
  public static final JsonNull INSTANCE = new JsonNull()

  /* Gson's add and addProperty overloads take a Java null to mean the JSON null
     element, and the value they store is JsonNull.INSTANCE. The element-typed
     branch has to come first in the conditional: an expression's type is its
     first branch's, so `value == null ? JsonNull.INSTANCE : value` would be a
     JsonNull and refuse to convert the value into one. */
  static JsonElement orNull(JsonElement value) {
    return value != null ? value : JsonNull.INSTANCE
  }

  public JsonNull() {
  }

  public JsonElement deepCopy() {
    return INSTANCE
  }

  public boolean equals(Object other) {
    return other instanceof JsonNull
  }

  public int hashCode() {
    return 1
  }
}

/* The numeric conversions every accessor goes through.

   None of the wrapper classes' parse methods can be used for this: the runtime
   behind Integer.parseInt is strtoll without an end pointer and without a
   range check, so it answers 0 for "abc" (rather than throwing) and quietly
   truncates "99999999999999". A library that returns 0 for a field the document
   never held is worse than one that fails, so the text is validated here and
   every failure throws. */
class JsonNumbers {
  private static final long MAX_LONG = 9223372036854775807L

  public static boolean isDigit(int c) {
    return c >= 48 && c <= 57
  }

  // true for the text of an integral number: an optional sign and digits, and
  // nothing else (Java's parseInt/parseLong grammar, which rejects "1.0" and
  // " 13 " and accepts leading zeros)
  public static boolean isIntegerText(String s) {
    int n = s.length()
    if (n == 0) {
      return false
    }
    int i = 0
    if (s.charAt(0) == '-' || s.charAt(0) == '+') {
      i = 1
    }
    if (i >= n) {
      return false
    }
    for ( : i < n : i++) {
      if (!isDigit((int) s.charAt(i))) {
        return false
      }
    }
    return true
  }

  public static int parseInt(String s) {
    long v = parseLong(s)
    if (v > 2147483647L || v < -2147483648L) {
      throw forInputString(s)
    }
    return (int) v
  }

  public static long parseLong(String s) {
    if (!isIntegerText(s)) {
      throw forInputString(s)
    }
    boolean negative = s.charAt(0) == '-'
    int from = s.length() > 0 && (s.charAt(0) == '-' || s.charAt(0) == '+') ? 1 : 0
    /* -9223372036854775808 is one past Long.MAX_VALUE in magnitude, so it is
       the one text the general bound below cannot accept and it is recognised
       by name. */
    if (negative && s.equals("-9223372036854775808")) {
      return -MAX_LONG - 1
    }
    long acc = 0
    for (int i = from : i < s.length() : i++) {
      long d = (long) ((int) s.charAt(i) - 48)
      if (acc > (MAX_LONG - d) / 10L) {
        throw forInputString(s)
      }
      acc = acc * 10L + d
    }
    if (negative) {
      return -acc
    }
    return acc
  }

  // Gson's getAsBoolean falls back to Boolean.parseBoolean, whose contract is
  // "true ignoring case". The one in the standard library compares with strcmp,
  // so "TRUE" would come back false; the case folding is done here instead of
  // letting a caller see the difference.
  public static boolean parseBoolean(String s) {
    if (s.length() != 4) {
      return false
    }
    return lower(s.charAt(0)) == 't' && lower(s.charAt(1)) == 'r' &&
      lower(s.charAt(2)) == 'u' && lower(s.charAt(3)) == 'e'
  }

  private static char lower(char c) {
    if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') {
      return (char) ((int) c + 32)
    }
    return c
  }

  /* Java's Double.parseDouble, minus the hexadecimal form ("0x1p3") and minus
     the distinctions it draws between a value and its neighbours: the text is
     validated here and the conversion itself is left to the runtime's strtod,
     which rounds to nearest like Java's. */
  public static double parseDouble(String s) {
    int from = 0
    int to = s.length()
    while (from < to && isSpace(s.charAt(from))) {
      from++
    }
    while (to > from && isSpace(s.charAt(to - 1))) {
      to--
    }
    String t = s.substring(from, to)
    if (!isNumberText(t)) {
      throw forInputString(s)
    }
    return Double.parseDouble(t)
  }

  private static boolean isSpace(char c) {
    return c == ' ' || c == '\t' || c == '\n' || c == '\r' || c == '\f' || c == ''
  }

  /* The number grammar of Double.parseDouble, and of Gson's lenient reader:
     an optional sign, digits on either side of a point (so ".5" and "1." are
     numbers), an optional exponent, the f/d suffix, and the two words the double
     grammar has, NaN and Infinity. It is public because the lenient reader in
     this file is what decides whether a bare token is a number or Gson's
     unquoted string, and the same grammar is what makes that decision there. */
  public static boolean isNumberText(String s) {
    int n = s.length()
    if (n == 0) {
      return false
    }
    String body = s
    if (lower(body.charAt(0)) == 'n') {
      return lowercase(body).equals("nan")
    }
    int i = 0
    if (body.charAt(0) == '-' || body.charAt(0) == '+') {
      i = 1
    }
    if (i < n && lower(body.charAt(i)) == 'i') {
      return lowercase(body.substring(i, n)).equals("infinity")
    }
    int digits = 0
    while (i < n && isDigit((int) body.charAt(i))) {
      digits++
      i++
    }
    if (i < n && body.charAt(i) == '.') {
      i++
      while (i < n && isDigit((int) body.charAt(i))) {
        digits++
        i++
      }
    }
    if (digits == 0) {
      return false
    }
    if (i < n && (body.charAt(i) == 'e' || body.charAt(i) == 'E')) {
      i++
      if (i < n && (body.charAt(i) == '+' || body.charAt(i) == '-')) {
        i++
      }
      int exponentDigits = 0
      while (i < n && isDigit((int) body.charAt(i))) {
        exponentDigits++
        i++
      }
      if (exponentDigits == 0) {
        return false
      }
    }
    // Java accepts the f/d suffix on a double literal
    if (i < n && (lower(body.charAt(i)) == 'f' || lower(body.charAt(i)) == 'd')) {
      i++
    }
    return i == n
  }

  private static String lowercase(String s) {
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder()
    for (int i = 0 : i < s.length() : i++) {
      sb.append(lower(s.charAt(i)))
    }
    return sb.toString()
  }

  /* Java's (int) and (long) casts on a double truncate toward zero and clamp at
     the ends of the range. The generated C for the cast itself does neither --
     an out-of-range conversion is whatever the hardware's cvttsd2si leaves
     behind, which is Integer.MIN_VALUE -- so the range is checked here. */
  public static int intOf(double v) {
    if (v != v) {
      return 0
    }
    if (v >= 2147483647.0) {
      return 2147483647
    }
    if (v <= -2147483648.0) {
      return -2147483648
    }
    return (int) v
  }

  public static long longOf(double v) {
    if (v != v) {
      return 0
    }
    if (v >= 9.223372036854775807E18) {
      return MAX_LONG
    }
    if (v <= -9.223372036854775808E18) {
      return -MAX_LONG - 1
    }
    return (long) v
  }

  // "For input string: \"12x\"" -- Java's NumberFormatException message
  public static IllegalArgumentException forInputString(String s) {
    return new IllegalArgumentException("For input string: \"" + s + "\"")
  }
}

/* com.google.gson.stream.JsonWriter, the subset the tree writers need.

   It exists as a separate class rather than as loops inside JsonObject and
   Gson because the separators, the indentation and the escape table all depend
   on the writer's configuration, and Gson's rendering of a tree is defined by
   this state machine: a name is written by the call that opens its value, so
   that when a null member is dropped under serializeNulls=false nothing is
   left dangling. */
class JsonWriter {
  private static final int EMPTY_ARRAY = 1
  private static final int NONEMPTY_ARRAY = 2
  private static final int EMPTY_OBJECT = 3
  private static final int DANGLING_NAME = 4
  private static final int NONEMPTY_OBJECT = 5
  private static final int EMPTY_DOCUMENT = 6
  private static final int NONEMPTY_DOCUMENT = 7

  private StringBuilder out
  private String indent
  private String separator
  private boolean htmlSafe
  private boolean serializeNulls
  private int[] stack
  private int top
  private String deferredName

  public JsonWriter(StringBuilder out) {
    this.out = out
    separator = ":"
    serializeNulls = true
    stack = new int[32]
    top = 0
    stack[0] = EMPTY_DOCUMENT
  }

  // "" turns indentation off, anything else turns it on and widens the name
  // separator to ": ", exactly as in Gson
  public void setIndent(String indent) {
    if (indent.length() == 0) {
      this.indent = null
      separator = ":"
      return
    }
    this.indent = indent
    separator = ": "
  }

  public void setHtmlSafe(boolean htmlSafe) {
    this.htmlSafe = htmlSafe
  }

  public void setSerializeNulls(boolean serializeNulls) {
    this.serializeNulls = serializeNulls
  }

  public void beginObject() {
    writeDeferredName()
    open(EMPTY_OBJECT, '{')
  }

  public void endObject() {
    close(EMPTY_OBJECT, NONEMPTY_OBJECT, '}')
  }

  public void beginArray() {
    writeDeferredName()
    open(EMPTY_ARRAY, '[')
  }

  public void endArray() {
    close(EMPTY_ARRAY, NONEMPTY_ARRAY, ']')
  }

  public void name(String name) {
    deferredName = name
  }

  public void value(String value) {
    writeDeferredName()
    beforeValue()
    escape(value)
  }

  public void value(Number value) {
    if (value == null) {
      nullValue()
      return
    }
    writeDeferredName()
    beforeValue()
    out.append(value.toString())
  }

  public void value(boolean value) {
    writeDeferredName()
    beforeValue()
    out.append(value)
  }

  public void nullValue() {
    if (deferredName != null) {
      if (serializeNulls) {
        writeDeferredName()
      } else {
        // the member is skipped whole: the name was never written, so there is
        // no punctuation to undo
        deferredName = null
        return
      }
    }
    beforeValue()
    out.append("null")
  }

  // The document written so far, which is what the sink holds: Gson's writer
  // takes a java.io.Writer and has no such call, and this one is handed the
  // StringBuilder it appends to, so the text can be read back from either.
  public String toString() {
    return out.toString()
  }

  /* Gson's TypeAdapters.JSON_ELEMENT adapter, which Streams.write delegates to:
     the whole tree in one place, so that JsonElement.toString() and
     Gson.toJson() cannot drift apart. */
  public static void write(JsonElement element, JsonWriter writer) {
    if (element == null || element.isJsonNull()) {
      writer.nullValue()
      return
    }
    if (element.isJsonPrimitive()) {
      writePrimitive((JsonPrimitive) element, writer)
      return
    }
    if (element.isJsonArray()) {
      JsonArray array = (JsonArray) element
      writer.beginArray()
      for (JsonElement e : array) {
        write(e, writer)
      }
      writer.endArray()
      return
    }
    if (element.isJsonObject()) {
      JsonObject object = (JsonObject) element
      writer.beginObject()
      for (JsonMember member : object.entrySet()) {
        writer.name(member.getKey())
        write(member.getValue(), writer)
      }
      writer.endObject()
      return
    }
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("unknown JsonElement type: " + element.getClass().getName())
  }

  private static void writePrimitive(JsonPrimitive value, JsonWriter writer) {
    if (value.isBoolean()) {
      writer.value(value.getAsBoolean())
      return
    }
    if (value.isNumber()) {
      writer.value(value.getAsNumber())
      return
    }
    writer.value(value.getAsString())
  }

  private void open(int empty, char bracket) {
    beforeValue()
    push(empty)
    out.append(bracket)
  }

  private void close(int empty, int nonempty, char bracket) {
    int context = stack[top]
    if (context != nonempty && context != empty) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("Nesting problem")
    }
    if (deferredName != null) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("Dangling name: " + deferredName)
    }
    top--
    if (context == nonempty) {
      newline()
    }
    out.append(bracket)
  }

  private void push(int context) {
    if (top + 1 == stack.length) {
      int[] bigger = new int[stack.length * 2]
      for (int i = 0 : i < stack.length : i++) {
        bigger[i] = stack[i]
      }
      stack = bigger
    }
    top++
    stack[top] = context
  }

  private void writeDeferredName() {
    if (deferredName == null) {
      return
    }
    beforeName()
    escape(deferredName)
    deferredName = null
  }

  private void beforeName() {
    int context = stack[top]
    if (context == NONEMPTY_OBJECT) {
      out.append(',')
    } else if (context != EMPTY_OBJECT) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("Nesting problem")
    }
    newline()
    stack[top] = DANGLING_NAME
  }

  private void beforeValue() {
    int context = stack[top]
    if (context == NONEMPTY_DOCUMENT) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("JSON must have only one top-level value")
    } else if (context == EMPTY_DOCUMENT) {
      stack[top] = NONEMPTY_DOCUMENT
    } else if (context == EMPTY_ARRAY) {
      stack[top] = NONEMPTY_ARRAY
      newline()
    } else if (context == NONEMPTY_ARRAY) {
      out.append(',')
      newline()
    } else if (context == DANGLING_NAME) {
      out.append(separator)
      stack[top] = NONEMPTY_OBJECT
    } else {
      throw new IllegalStateException("Nesting problem")
    }
  }

  private void newline() {
    if (indent == null) {
      return
    }
    out.append('\n')
    // one level per open container; the document itself is not one
    for (int i = 1 : i < top + 1 : i++) {
      out.append(indent)
    }
  }

  /* Gson's replacement table, applied byte by byte. Strings here are UTF-8, so
     a byte above 0x7f is never a character on its own: it is part of a
     multi-byte sequence that has to be copied verbatim (which substring does)
     rather than re-appended as a char, which would encode the byte again. */
  private void escape(String value) {
    int length = value.length()
    out.append('"')
    int last = 0
    for (int i = 0 : i < length : i++) {
      int c = (int) value.charAt(i)
      if (c >= 128) {
        /* U+2028 and U+2029 are the only non-ASCII characters Gson escapes:
           valid JSON, but a line break if the text later ends up inside a
           <script>. They are E2 80 A8 and E2 80 A9 here. */
        if (c == 0xe2 && i + 2 < length && (int) value.charAt(i + 1) == 0x80) {
          int third = (int) value.charAt(i + 2)
          if (third == 0xa8 || third == 0xa9) {
            out.append(value.substring(last, i))
            out.append(third == 0xa8 ? "\\u2028" : "\\u2029")
            i += 2
            last = i + 1
          }
        }
        continue
      }
      String replacement = replacementFor(c)
      if (replacement == null) {
        continue
      }
      out.append(value.substring(last, i))
      out.append(replacement)
      last = i + 1
    }
    if (last < length) {
      out.append(value.substring(last, length))
    }
    out.append('"')
  }

  private String replacementFor(int c) {
    if (c == '"') {
      return "\\\""
    }
    if (c == '\\') {
      return "\\\\"
    }
    if (c == '\b') {
      return "\\b"
    }
    if (c == '\t') {
      return "\\t"
    }
    if (c == '\n') {
      return "\\n"
    }
    if (c == '\f') {
      return "\\f"
    }
    if (c == '\r') {
      return "\\r"
    }
    if (c < 0x20) {
      // "\u00xx" style, lower-case hex, as String.format("%04x") prints it
      StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder()
      sb.append("\\u00")
      sb.append(hexDigit((c >> 4) & 15))
      sb.append(hexDigit(c & 15))
      return sb.toString()
    }
    if (htmlSafe) {
      // Gson escapes these only in its default (html-safe) configuration, so
      // new Gson().toJson() differs from element.toString() on exactly them
      if (c == '<') {
        return "\\u003c"
      }
      if (c == '>') {
        return "\\u003e"
      }
      if (c == '&') {
        return "\\u0026"
      }
      if (c == '=') {
        return "\\u003d"
      }
      if (c == '\'') {
        return "\\u0027"
      }
    }
    return null
  }

  private static char hexDigit(int v) {
    if (v < 10) {
      return (char) (48 + v)
    }
    return (char) (87 + v)
  }
}

/* A recursive-descent reader for one document. Strict on purpose: this is the
   RFC 8259 grammar and nothing else (see the header for what Gson's lenient
   reader would have accepted instead).

   The nesting limit is a bound the format is read under rather than a stand-in
   for one: this reader recurses, and a document nested past the limit is
   refused with a JsonSyntaxException instead of a StackOverflowError, which
   keeps a hostile document from spending the whole stack -- the error is
   catchable now (the runtime throws it from the check every generated function
   begins with), but a reader that answers "too deep" is more useful than one
   that answers "the machine ran out". 255 is the default Gson 2.11 gives its
   JsonReader, so a document this refuses is one Gson refuses too. */
class JsonReader {
  private static final int MAX_DEPTH = 255
  private static final String REPLACEMENT = "?"

  private String src
  private int pos
  private int depth
  private boolean lenient

  /* The token layer's own state, which parseDocument does not touch: the
     containers that are open (two ints each, the kind and the values read), the
     token peek answered, and what that token carries. */
  private static final int DOCUMENT = 1
  private static final int ARRAY = 2
  private static final int OBJECT = 3

  private static final int FRAMES = 16

  private int[] frames
  private int open
  private String peeked
  private String tokenText
  private boolean tokenFlag
  private boolean named

  public JsonReader(String src) {
    this.src = src
    frames = new int[FRAMES * 2]
    frames[0] = DOCUMENT
    open = 1
  }

  /* setLenient opens the door to the documents Gson's lenient reader takes and
     RFC 8259 does not: an unquoted name or string ({a:1}, 'a', abc), a trailing
     comma before a closing bracket, a number written loosely (01, +1, .5, 1.,
     NaN, Infinity), a comment, and -- at the document level -- an empty
     document, which Gson answers with JsonNull.INSTANCE.

     The switch is the reader's own, so nothing a program does makes
     JsonParser.parseString lenient; the binding turns it on for the readers it
     builds when the Gson it was handed asked for it with setLenient(true). */
  public void setLenient(boolean lenient) {
    this.lenient = lenient
  }

  public boolean isLenient() {
    return lenient
  }

  /* ---- streaming: Gson's JsonReader, one token at a time ----------------

     A tree answers a document that fits in memory and is wanted whole; a token
     stream answers one read once, from front to back, holding nothing but the
     containers that are open -- which is the shape Gson's JsonReader has, and
     the one a document too large for a tree is read with:

       while (in.hasNext()) { in.nextName() ... }

     This reader holds the text it was built from, so what the token layer saves
     here is the tree and not the text. The scanner underneath is the same one
     parseDocument uses -- skipWhitespace, parseName, parseStringLiteral,
     parseNumberLiteral -- so the two agree on the grammar, on what lenient
     accepts, and on what a failure says.

     A document is read once, one way or the other: parseDocument answers the
     whole text as a JsonElement and the calls below consume it token by token,
     and a program that mixes them continues from wherever the last call left
     the scan. */

  // The tokens, spelled as Gson's JsonToken names them. peek answers one of
  // these and the accessors below refuse a call that does not match it.
  public static final String BEGIN_OBJECT = "BEGIN_OBJECT"
  public static final String END_OBJECT = "END_OBJECT"
  public static final String BEGIN_ARRAY = "BEGIN_ARRAY"
  public static final String END_ARRAY = "END_ARRAY"
  public static final String NAME = "NAME"
  public static final String STRING = "STRING"
  public static final String NUMBER = "NUMBER"
  public static final String BOOLEAN = "BOOLEAN"
  public static final String NULL = "NULL"
  public static final String END_DOCUMENT = "END_DOCUMENT"

  /* peek is the type of the next token. The token is read once and kept until
     an accessor takes it -- a second peek answers the same token rather than
     reading another -- and the separator before it, a comma between elements or
     the colon after a name, is consumed here. */
  public String peek() {
    if (peeked == null) {
      scan()
    }
    return peeked
  }

  // hasNext: another element in the array or the object being read, or another
  // value at the document level. It is what a loop over a container asks.
  public boolean hasNext() {
    String t = peek()
    return !t.equals(END_ARRAY) && !t.equals(END_OBJECT) && !t.equals(END_DOCUMENT)
  }

  public void beginObject() {
    take(BEGIN_OBJECT)
    push(OBJECT)
  }

  public void endObject() {
    take(END_OBJECT)
    pop(OBJECT)
  }

  public void beginArray() {
    take(BEGIN_ARRAY)
    push(ARRAY)
  }

  public void endArray() {
    take(END_ARRAY)
    pop(ARRAY)
  }

  // nextName is an object member's name, which is the token the reader is on
  // when an object has another member.
  public String nextName() {
    String t = peek()
    if (!t.equals(NAME)) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("expected a name but was " + t)
    }
    peeked = null
    return tokenText
  }

  // nextString is a string, and a number too: Gson's reader answers a number's
  // text for it, which is how a String member takes the number a document
  // carries without a second spelling of the call.
  public String nextString() {
    String t = peek()
    if (!t.equals(STRING) && !t.equals(NUMBER)) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("expected a string but was " + t)
    }
    peeked = null
    return tokenText
  }

  public long nextLong() {
    String text = number("long", true)
    try {
      return JsonNumbers.parseLong(text)
    } catch (IllegalArgumentException ex) {
      // an integer literal the type cannot hold, which Gson refuses the same way
      throw new IllegalStateException("expected a long but was " + text)
    }
  }

  public int nextInt() {
    return (int) nextLong()
  }

  public double nextDouble() {
    return JsonNumbers.parseDouble(number("double", false))
  }

  public boolean nextBoolean() {
    String t = peek()
    if (!t.equals(BOOLEAN)) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("expected a boolean but was " + t)
    }
    peeked = null
    return tokenFlag
  }

  public void nextNull() {
    String t = peek()
    if (!t.equals(NULL)) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("expected null but was " + t)
    }
    peeked = null
  }

  /* skipValue is the value the reader is on, whatever it is, dropped without
     being looked at -- the call a program makes for a member it does not want,
     which is what reading a document for a few of its members costs instead of
     building all of it. A container is walked the same way, so the reader ends
     up past the whole value. */
  public void skipValue() {
    String t = peek()
    if (t.equals(BEGIN_OBJECT)) {
      beginObject()
      while (hasNext()) {
        nextName()
        skipValue()
      }
      endObject()
      return
    }
    if (t.equals(BEGIN_ARRAY)) {
      beginArray()
      while (hasNext()) {
        skipValue()
      }
      endArray()
      return
    }
    if (t.equals(STRING) || t.equals(NUMBER)) {
      nextString()
      return
    }
    if (t.equals(BOOLEAN)) {
      nextBoolean()
      return
    }
    if (t.equals(NULL)) {
      nextNull()
      return
    }
    throw new IllegalStateException("expected a value but was " + t)
  }

  /* number is the text of the number the reader is on, refused when the reader
     is on anything else. A whole-number accessor also refuses a literal with a
     fraction or an exponent, which is Gson's "Expected an int but was 1.5"
     rather than a rounding the caller did not ask for. */
  private String number(String want, boolean whole) {
    String t = peek()
    if (!t.equals(NUMBER)) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("expected a " + want + " but was " + t)
    }
    if (whole && !JsonNumbers.isIntegerText(tokenText)) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("expected a whole number but was " + tokenText)
    }
    peeked = null
    return tokenText
  }

  // take consumes the token the caller's call is for and refuses a call that
  // does not match the document.
  private void take(String want) {
    String t = peek()
    if (!t.equals(want)) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("expected " + want + " but was " + t)
    }
    peeked = null
  }

  /* scan reads the next token and leaves it as the peeked one: the separator
     that precedes a token is taken here, the token itself is not (an accessor
     consumes it), and the frame the token belongs to is updated as the token is
     read -- a value is counted where it belongs, a name leaves the object
     waiting for its value. */
  private void scan() {
    skipWhitespace()
    if (named) {
      // the value of the name just read
      named = false
      count()
      peeked = scanValue()
      return
    }
    int kind = frames[2 * (open - 1)]
    int read = frames[2 * (open - 1) + 1]
    if (kind == DOCUMENT) {
      if (read > 0) {
        if (pos >= src.length()) {
          peeked = END_DOCUMENT
          return
        }
        throw error("did not consume the entire document")
      }
      count()
      peeked = scanValue()
      return
    }
    char closer = kind == ARRAY ? ']' : '}'
    if (pos >= src.length()) {
      throw error(kind == ARRAY ? "unterminated array" : "unterminated object")
    }
    if (read > 0) {
      // an element has been read, so what comes next is the comma before
      // another one, or the bracket that closes the container
      char c = src.charAt(pos)
      if (c == closer) {
        pos++
        peeked = kind == ARRAY ? END_ARRAY : END_OBJECT
        return
      }
      if (c != ',') {
        throw error("expected ',' or '" + closer + "' but was '" + c + "'")
      }
      pos++
      skipWhitespace()
      if (pos >= src.length()) {
        throw error(kind == ARRAY ? "unterminated array" : "unterminated object")
      }
      // A trailing comma is what a lenient reader takes here; a strict one falls
      // through to the value (or the name) it was promised and refuses there,
      // which is the message parseArray and parseObject give for the same
      // document.
      if (lenient && src.charAt(pos) == closer) {
        pos++
        peeked = kind == ARRAY ? END_ARRAY : END_OBJECT
        return
      }
    } else if (src.charAt(pos) == closer) {
      // nothing read yet: the bracket closes an empty container
      pos++
      peeked = kind == ARRAY ? END_ARRAY : END_OBJECT
      return
    }
    if (kind == ARRAY) {
      count()
      peeked = scanValue()
      return
    }
    tokenText = parseName()
    skipWhitespace()
    if (pos >= src.length() || src.charAt(pos) != ':') {
      throw error("expected ':' after the name")
    }
    pos++
    named = true
    peeked = NAME
  }

  // scanValue is one value's token: a container's opening bracket, or a
  // scalar, which is read whole and kept in tokenText or tokenFlag.
  private String scanValue() {
    if (pos >= src.length()) {
      throw error("expected a value but was the end of the document")
    }
    char c = src.charAt(pos)
    if (c == '{') {
      pos++
      return BEGIN_OBJECT
    }
    if (c == '[') {
      pos++
      return BEGIN_ARRAY
    }
    if (c == '"') {
      tokenText = parseStringLiteral()
      return STRING
    }
    if (lenient) {
      return scanLenientValue(c)
    }
    if (c == 't') {
      expect("true")
      tokenFlag = true
      return BOOLEAN
    }
    if (c == 'f') {
      expect("false")
      tokenFlag = false
      return BOOLEAN
    }
    if (c == 'n') {
      expect("null")
      return NULL
    }
    if (c == '-' || JsonNumbers.isDigit((int) c)) {
      tokenText = parseNumberLiteral()
      return NUMBER
    }
    throw error("expected a value but was '" + c + "'")
  }

  // scanLenientValue is scanValue's lenient half, the same one
  // parseLenientValue takes: a single-quoted string, one of the three words, a
  // loosely written number, or an unquoted string -- which is a STRING, since
  // Gson reads an unquoted word as a string too.
  private String scanLenientValue(char c) {
    if (c == '\'') {
      tokenText = parseStringLiteral()
      return STRING
    }
    tokenText = scanBare()
    if (tokenText.length() == 0) {
      throw error("expected a value but was '" + c + "'")
    }
    if (tokenText.equals("true")) {
      tokenFlag = true
      return BOOLEAN
    }
    if (tokenText.equals("false")) {
      tokenFlag = false
      return BOOLEAN
    }
    if (tokenText.equals("null")) {
      return NULL
    }
    if (JsonNumbers.isNumberText(tokenText)) {
      return NUMBER
    }
    return STRING
  }

  // count is one more element read in the container the reader is in.
  private void count() {
    frames[2 * (open - 1) + 1]++
  }

  private void push(int kind) {
    // open counts the document frame too, and the limit counts containers.
    if (open - 1 >= MAX_DEPTH) {
      throw error("nesting depth limit of " + MAX_DEPTH + " exceeded")
    }
    if (2 * (open + 1) > frames.length) {
      int[] bigger = new int[frames.length * 2]
      for (int i = 0 : i < frames.length : i++) {
        bigger[i] = frames[i]
      }
      frames = bigger
    }
    frames[2 * open] = kind
    frames[2 * open + 1] = 0
    open++
  }

  // pop closes the container the reader is in, which has to be the one the call
  // is for: endObject inside an array is a caller that has lost its place.
  private void pop(int kind) {
    if (open <= 1 || frames[2 * (open - 1)] != kind) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("not inside " + (kind == OBJECT ? "an object" : "an array"))
    }
    open--
  }

  public JsonElement parseDocument() {
    skipWhitespace()
    if (pos >= src.length()) {
      if (lenient) {
        // Gson's JsonParser answers JsonNull.INSTANCE for a document with
        // nothing in it, which is the one place its reader does not fail
        return JsonNull.INSTANCE
      }
      throw error("empty document")
    }
    JsonElement element = parseValue()
    skipWhitespace()
    if (pos < src.length()) {
      // Gson's wording for this one, since it is the check callers notice
      throw error("did not consume the entire document")
    }
    return element
  }

  private JsonElement parseValue() {
    char c = src.charAt(pos)
    if (c == '{') {
      return parseObject()
    }
    if (c == '[') {
      return parseArray()
    }
    if (c == '"') {
      return new JsonPrimitive(parseStringLiteral())
    }
    if (lenient) {
      return parseLenientValue()
    }
    if (c == 't') {
      expect("true")
      return new JsonPrimitive(Boolean.valueOf(true))
    }
    if (c == 'f') {
      expect("false")
      return new JsonPrimitive(Boolean.valueOf(false))
    }
    if (c == 'n') {
      expect("null")
      return JsonNull.INSTANCE
    }
    if (c == '-' || JsonNumbers.isDigit((int) c)) {
      // the literal text is the number, so that it prints back unchanged
      return new JsonPrimitive(new JsonLazyNumber(parseNumberLiteral()))
    }
    throw error("expected a value but was '" + c + "'")
    return JsonNull.INSTANCE
  }

  /* A value Gson's lenient reader takes: a single-quoted string, one of the
     three words, a loosely written number, or an unquoted string -- which Gson
     reads as a string too (its PEEKED_UNQUOTED is JsonToken.STRING), so
     `{a:abc}` carries "abc". */
  private JsonElement parseLenientValue() {
    char c = src.charAt(pos)
    if (c == '\'') {
      return new JsonPrimitive(parseStringLiteral())
    }
    String text = scanBare()
    if (text.length() == 0) {
      throw error("expected a value but was '" + c + "'")
    }
    if (text.equals("true")) {
      return new JsonPrimitive(Boolean.valueOf(true))
    }
    if (text.equals("false")) {
      return new JsonPrimitive(Boolean.valueOf(false))
    }
    if (text.equals("null")) {
      return JsonNull.INSTANCE
    }
    if (JsonNumbers.isNumberText(text)) {
      // the text is kept, as it is for a strict number, so NaN and 1. print
      // back the way they were written
      return new JsonPrimitive(new JsonLazyNumber(text))
    }
    return new JsonPrimitive(text)
  }

  // scanBare is one unquoted token: everything up to the character that ends it
  // -- a bracket, a comma, a colon or whitespace. It answers "" when the
  // character under the cursor ends the token before it starts, which is how a
  // caller tells "no name here" from a name.
  private String scanBare() {
    int start = pos
    while (pos < src.length()) {
      char c = src.charAt(pos)
      if (c == ',' || c == ':' || c == '}' || c == ']' || c == ' ' || c == '\t' ||
        c == '\n' || c == '\r') {
        break
      }
      pos++
    }
    return src.substring(start, pos)
  }

  /* A member's name: a quoted string, and in lenient mode the single-quoted or
     unquoted form Gson takes too. */
  private String parseName() {
    if (pos >= src.length()) {
      throw error("expected a name")
    }
    char c = src.charAt(pos)
    if (c == '"') {
      return parseStringLiteral()
    }
    if (!lenient) {
      throw error("expected a name")
    }
    if (c == '\'') {
      return parseStringLiteral()
    }
    String bare = scanBare()
    if (bare.length() == 0) {
      throw error("expected a name")
    }
    return bare
  }

  private JsonElement parseObject() {
    enter()
    pos++
    JsonObject object = new JsonObject()
    skipWhitespace()
    if (pos < src.length() && src.charAt(pos) == '}') {
      pos++
      depth--
      return object
    }
    while (true) {
      skipWhitespace()
      String name = parseName()
      skipWhitespace()
      if (pos >= src.length() || src.charAt(pos) != ':') {
        throw error("expected ':' after the name")
      }
      pos++
      skipWhitespace()
      if (pos >= src.length()) {
        throw error("expected a value after ':'")
      }
      // a repeated name replaces the value and keeps the first position, which
      // is what Gson's map does
      object.add(name, parseValue())
      skipWhitespace()
      if (pos >= src.length()) {
        throw error("unterminated object")
      }
      char c = src.charAt(pos)
      if (c == ',') {
        pos++
        if (lenient) {
          // a trailing comma is one of the things Gson's lenient reader takes
          skipWhitespace()
          if (pos < src.length() && src.charAt(pos) == '}') {
            pos++
            depth--
            return object
          }
        }
        continue
      }
      if (c == '}') {
        pos++
        depth--
        return object
      }
      throw error("expected ',' or '}' but was '" + c + "'")
    }
    // unreachable for the same reason as the accessors above: the loop exits
    // only through a return or a throw
    return object
  }

  private JsonElement parseArray() {
    enter()
    pos++
    JsonArray array = new JsonArray()
    skipWhitespace()
    if (pos < src.length() && src.charAt(pos) == ']') {
      pos++
      depth--
      return array
    }
    while (true) {
      skipWhitespace()
      if (pos >= src.length()) {
        throw error("unterminated array")
      }
      array.add(parseValue())
      skipWhitespace()
      if (pos >= src.length()) {
        throw error("unterminated array")
      }
      char c = src.charAt(pos)
      if (c == ',') {
        pos++
        if (lenient) {
          skipWhitespace()
          if (pos < src.length() && src.charAt(pos) == ']') {
            pos++
            depth--
            return array
          }
        }
        continue
      }
      if (c == ']') {
        pos++
        depth--
        return array
      }
      throw error("expected ',' or ']' but was '" + c + "'")
    }
    return array
  }

  /* The number is kept as text: JsonLazyNumber holds it until someone asks for
     a numeric type, so 1.00 prints back as 1.00. The grammar is JSON's, which
     is stricter than Java's parseDouble (no leading zero, no leading +, no
     ".5", no "1."). */
  private String parseNumberLiteral() {
    int start = pos
    if (src.charAt(pos) == '-') {
      pos++
      if (pos >= src.length()) {
        throw error("expected a digit after '-'")
      }
    }
    if (src.charAt(pos) == '0') {
      pos++
    } else if (JsonNumbers.isDigit((int) src.charAt(pos))) {
      while (pos < src.length() && JsonNumbers.isDigit((int) src.charAt(pos))) {
        pos++
      }
    } else {
      throw error("expected a digit")
    }
    if (pos < src.length() && src.charAt(pos) == '.') {
      pos++
      if (pos >= src.length() || !JsonNumbers.isDigit((int) src.charAt(pos))) {
        throw error("expected a digit after '.'")
      }
      while (pos < src.length() && JsonNumbers.isDigit((int) src.charAt(pos))) {
        pos++
      }
    }
    if (pos < src.length() && (src.charAt(pos) == 'e' || src.charAt(pos) == 'E')) {
      pos++
      if (pos < src.length() && (src.charAt(pos) == '+' || src.charAt(pos) == '-')) {
        pos++
      }
      if (pos >= src.length() || !JsonNumbers.isDigit((int) src.charAt(pos))) {
        throw error("expected a digit in the exponent")
      }
      while (pos < src.length() && JsonNumbers.isDigit((int) src.charAt(pos))) {
        pos++
      }
    }
    return src.substring(start, pos)
  }

  /* Everything between the quotes is UTF-8, so the bytes of a multi-byte
     character are copied with substring and never re-appended one by one; only
     escapes are decoded. A single quote opens a string too when the reader is
     lenient, which is Gson's other spelling of a string; the two are not
     interchangeable inside one literal -- `'a"` is unterminated. */
  private String parseStringLiteral() {
    char quote = src.charAt(pos)
    pos++
    int start = pos
    StringBuilder sb = null
    while (true) {
      if (pos >= src.length()) {
        throw error("unterminated string")
      }
      int c = (int) src.charAt(pos)
      if (c == quote) {
        break
      }
      if (c == '\\') {
        if (sb == null) {
          sb = new StringBuilder()
        }
        sb.append(src.substring(start, pos))
        appendEscape(sb)
        start = pos
        continue
      }
      if (c < 0x20) {
        throw error("unescaped control character in a string")
      }
      pos++
    }
    if (sb == null) {
      String text = src.substring(start, pos)
      pos++
      return text
    }
    sb.append(src.substring(start, pos))
    pos++
    return sb.toString()
  }

  private void appendEscape(StringBuilder sb) {
    pos++
    if (pos >= src.length()) {
      throw error("unterminated string")
    }
    char e = src.charAt(pos)
    if (e == '"') {
      pos++
      sb.append('"')
      return
    }
    if (e == '\'') {
      // `\'` is an escape Gson's lenient reader takes, and the only way to put
      // a single quote inside a single-quoted string
      pos++
      sb.append('\'')
      return
    }
    if (e == '\\') {
      pos++
      sb.append('\\')
      return
    }
    if (e == '/') {
      pos++
      sb.append('/')
      return
    }
    if (e == 'b') {
      pos++
      sb.append((char) 8)
      return
    }
    if (e == 'f') {
      pos++
      sb.append((char) 12)
      return
    }
    if (e == 'n') {
      pos++
      sb.append((char) 10)
      return
    }
    if (e == 'r') {
      pos++
      sb.append((char) 13)
      return
    }
    if (e == 't') {
      pos++
      sb.append((char) 9)
      return
    }
    if (e == 'u') {
      appendUnicodeEscape(sb)
      return
    }
    throw error("invalid escape sequence '\\" + e + "'")
  }

  /* \uXXXX, pairing a high surrogate with the low surrogate after it. A
     surrogate without a partner has no UTF-8 spelling that this language's
     16-bit char can produce (appendChar would encode it as CESU-8, which is
     not valid UTF-8), so it becomes U+FFFD. */
  private void appendUnicodeEscape(StringBuilder sb) {
    pos++
    int cp = readHex4()
    if (cp >= 0xd800 && cp <= 0xdbff) {
      if (pos + 1 < src.length() && src.charAt(pos) == '\\' && src.charAt(pos + 1) == 'u') {
        int save = pos
        pos += 2
        int low = readHex4()
        if (low >= 0xdc00 && low <= 0xdfff) {
          sb.append(encodeCodePoint(0x10000 + ((cp - 0xd800) << 10) + (low - 0xdc00)))
          return
        }
        pos = save
      }
      sb.append(REPLACEMENT)
      return
    }
    if (cp >= 0xdc00 && cp <= 0xdfff) {
      sb.append(REPLACEMENT)
      return
    }
    sb.append((char) cp)
  }

  private int readHex4() {
    int value = 0
    for (int i = 0 : i < 4 : i++) {
      if (pos >= src.length()) {
        throw error("incomplete \\u escape")
      }
      int digit = hexValue((int) src.charAt(pos))
      if (digit < 0) {
        throw error("invalid \\u escape")
      }
      value = (value << 4) | digit
      pos++
    }
    return value
  }

  private static int hexValue(int c) {
    if (c >= 48 && c <= 57) {
      return c - 48
    }
    if (c >= 65 && c <= 70) {
      return c - 55
    }
    if (c >= 97 && c <= 102) {
      return c - 87
    }
    return -1
  }

  /* A code point above the BMP cannot be appended as a char: char is 16 bits
     and StringBuilder.append(char) encodes its value, so a surrogate would come
     out as three bytes of CESU-8 instead of the four UTF-8 wants. The four bytes
     are written through String.replace, which substitutes single bytes, and the
     placeholder bytes 0x01-0x04 cannot collide with any of them: a UTF-8 lead
     or continuation byte is always 0x80 or above. */
  private static String encodeCodePoint(int cp) {
    String s = "\u0001\u0002\u0003\u0004"
    s = s.replace((char) 1, (char) (0xf0 | (cp >> 18)))
    s = s.replace((char) 2, (char) (0x80 | ((cp >> 12) & 0x3f)))
    s = s.replace((char) 3, (char) (0x80 | ((cp >> 6) & 0x3f)))
    s = s.replace((char) 4, (char) (0x80 | (cp & 0x3f)))
    return s
  }

  private void expect(String literal) {
    if (pos + literal.length() > src.length() || !src.substring(pos, pos + literal.length()).equals(literal)) {
      throw error("expected '" + literal + "'")
    }
    pos += literal.length()
  }

  private void enter() {
    if (depth >= MAX_DEPTH) {
      throw error("nesting depth limit of " + MAX_DEPTH + " exceeded")
    }
    depth++
  }

  private void skipWhitespace() {
    while (pos < src.length()) {
      char c = src.charAt(pos)
      if (c == ' ' || c == '\t' || c == '\n' || c == '\r') {
        pos++
        continue
      }
      if (lenient && c == '/' && pos + 1 < src.length()) {
        // a comment is whitespace to Gson's lenient reader, both spellings of it
        char second = src.charAt(pos + 1)
        if (second == '/') {
          pos += 2
          while (pos < src.length() && src.charAt(pos) != '\n') {
            pos++
          }
          continue
        }
        if (second == '*') {
          pos += 2
          while (pos + 1 < src.length() && !(src.charAt(pos) == '*' && src.charAt(pos + 1) == '/')) {
            pos++
          }
          if (pos + 1 >= src.length()) {
            throw error("unterminated comment")
          }
          pos += 2
          continue
        }
      }
      return
    }
  }

  /* The position is turned into a line and column only when something fails,
     which keeps the scan itself to one comparison per byte. */
  private JsonSyntaxException error(String what) {
    int line = 1
    int lineStart = 0
    for (int i = 0 : i < pos && i < src.length() : i++) {
      if (src.charAt(i) == '\n') {
        line++
        lineStart = i + 1
      }
    }
    return new JsonSyntaxException(what + " at line " + line + " column " + (pos - lineStart + 1))
  }
}

/* com.google.gson.JsonParser. */
class JsonParser {
  public static JsonElement parseString(String json) {
    if (json == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("json must not be null")
    }
    return new JsonReader(json).parseDocument()
  }
}

/* com.google.gson.FieldNamingPolicy: the five policies Gson has, and the
   translation each one makes of a field's name. The algorithm is Gson's,
   including the two that are easy to get subtly wrong: separateCamelCase
   inserts the separator before an upper-case letter that is not the first
   character, and upperCaseFirstLetter skips a leading run of characters that
   are not letters ("_name" becomes "_Name", not "Name"). */
enum FieldNamingPolicy {
  IDENTITY,
  UPPER_CAMEL_CASE,
  UPPER_CAMEL_CASE_WITH_SPACES,
  LOWER_CASE_WITH_UNDERSCORES,
  LOWER_CASE_WITH_DASHES:

  public String translateName(String name) {
    if (this == UPPER_CAMEL_CASE) {
      return upperCaseFirstLetter(name)
    }
    if (this == UPPER_CAMEL_CASE_WITH_SPACES) {
      return upperCaseFirstLetter(separateCamelCase(name, " "))
    }
    if (this == LOWER_CASE_WITH_UNDERSCORES) {
      return separateCamelCase(name, "_").toLowerCase()
    }
    if (this == LOWER_CASE_WITH_DASHES) {
      return separateCamelCase(name, "-").toLowerCase()
    }
    return name
  }

  public static String separateCamelCase(String name, String separator) {
    StringBuilder translation = new StringBuilder()
    for (int i = 0 : i < name.length() : i++) {
      char c = name.charAt(i)
      if (Character.isUpperCase(c) && translation.length() > 0) {
        translation.append(separator)
      }
      translation.append(c)
    }
    return translation.toString()
  }

  public static String upperCaseFirstLetter(String name) {
    StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder()
    int index = 0
    char first = name.charAt(index)
    while (index < name.length() - 1) {
      if (Character.isLetter(first)) {
        break
      }
      out.append(first)
      index++
      first = name.charAt(index)
    }
    if (!Character.isUpperCase(first)) {
      out.append(Character.toUpperCase(first))
      out.append(name.substring(index + 1, name.length()))
      return out.toString()
    }
    return name
  }
}

/* The adapters installed on a Gson, in registration order. A type half is
   matched by name and a hierarchy half by assignability, and the search runs
   backwards so that the last registration wins -- which is Gson's order, where
   the factories are inserted at the front of the list. */
class JsonAdapterList {
  private List<Class> types
  private List<Object> values
  private List<Boolean> hierarchies

  public JsonAdapterList() {
    types = new ArrayList<Class>()
    values = new ArrayList<Object>()
    hierarchies = new ArrayList<Boolean>()
  }

  public void add(Class type, Object adapter, boolean hierarchy) {
    types.add(type)
    values.add(adapter)
    hierarchies.add(hierarchy)
  }

  public Object find(Class type) {
    for (int i = types.size() - 1 : i >= 0 : i--) {
      if (hierarchies.get(i)) {
        if (types.get(i).isAssignableFrom(type)) {
          return values.get(i)
        }
        continue
      }
      if (types.get(i).equals(type)) {
        return values.get(i)
      }
    }
    return null
  }

  public boolean isEmpty() {
    return types.size() == 0
  }

  // copy is what makes a built Gson independent of its builder: the entries are
  // shared (an adapter is a stateless function in Gson's design) but the lists
  // are not, so registering on a builder afterwards cannot reach a Gson that
  // was already created.
  public JsonAdapterList copy() {
    JsonAdapterList out = new JsonAdapterList()
    for (int i = 0 : i < types.size() : i++) {
      out.add(types.get(i), values.get(i), hierarchies.get(i))
    }
    return out
  }
}

/* com.google.gson.Gson and its builder.

   Everything Gson is configured by is here, because the binding reads it:
   JsonBinding builds a reader and a writer per class from the configuration it
   is given (lib/27_json_binding.teyru), so what the builder accepts reaches the
   binding and two Gsons with different settings bind the same class
   differently. The defaults are Gson's, and the ones that show up in output are
   worth stating: a plain `new Gson()` escapes HTML characters (htmlSafe), drops
   null members (serializeNulls is false), excludes static and transient fields,
   names members after the field (IDENTITY) and ignores @Since and @Until
   (no version is set), where toString() on an element escapes nothing and keeps
   null members.

   One default is this library's own: lenient is false, so the documents a Gson
   reads are RFC 8259 and the deviations Gson's lenient reader allows are opt-in
   with setLenient(true). That is the strictness lib/10_json.teyru documents for
   the whole JSON layer. */
class Gson {
  private static final double IGNORE_VERSIONS = -1.0
  private static int sequence = 0

  private boolean prettyPrinting
  private boolean htmlSafe
  private boolean serializeNulls
  private boolean lenient
  private int excludeModifiers
  private boolean exposeOnly
  private FieldNamingPolicy naming
  private double version
  private JsonAdapterList adapters
  private Map<String, Object> resolved
  private int id

  public Gson() {
    prettyPrinting = false
    htmlSafe = true
    serializeNulls = false
    lenient = false
    // Gson's default exclusions, which excludeFieldsWithModifiers replaces
    excludeModifiers = Modifier.TRANSIENT | Modifier.STATIC
    exposeOnly = false
    naming = FieldNamingPolicy.IDENTITY
    version = IGNORE_VERSIONS
    adapters = new JsonAdapterList()
    resolved = new HashMap<String, Object>()
    sequence++
    id = sequence
  }

  Gson(Gson other) {
    prettyPrinting = other.prettyPrinting
    htmlSafe = other.htmlSafe
    serializeNulls = other.serializeNulls
    lenient = other.lenient
    excludeModifiers = other.excludeModifiers
    exposeOnly = other.exposeOnly
    naming = other.naming
    version = other.version
    adapters = other.adapters.copy()
    resolved = new HashMap<String, Object>()
    sequence++
    id = sequence
  }

  // ---- what the binding reads -------------------------------------------

  // identity is what a binding is cached under (see JsonBinding.key): the
  // configuration's own number, so that two Gsons that differ are two
  // bindings and a Gson that is copied is a third.
  public int identity() {
    return id
  }

  public boolean serializeNulls() {
    return serializeNulls
  }

  public boolean isLenient() {
    return lenient
  }

  public boolean prettyPrinting() {
    return prettyPrinting
  }

  public boolean htmlSafe() {
    return htmlSafe
  }

  // excludes is excludeFieldsWithModifiers: a field whose modifiers overlap the
  // set is not bound in either direction, as in Gson.
  public boolean excludes(int modifiers) {
    return (modifiers & excludeModifiers) != 0
  }

  public boolean exposeOnly() {
    return exposeOnly
  }

  // Gson's Excluder ignores the version annotations until setVersion names one,
  // and spells "none" as -1.
  public boolean versionSet() {
    return version != IGNORE_VERSIONS
  }

  public double version() {
    return version
  }

  public String translate(String name) {
    return naming.translateName(name)
  }

  public Object registeredAdapter(Class type) {
    if (adapters.isEmpty()) {
      return null
    }
    return adapters.find(type)
  }

  Object findAdapter(String name) {
    return resolved.get(name)
  }

  void rememberAdapter(String name, Object adapter) {
    resolved.put(name, adapter)
  }

  // ---- configured by GsonBuilder ----------------------------------------

  /* These eight are the builder's side of the configuration, written on the one
     Gson a builder holds until it hands out a copy. A program configures
     through GsonBuilder, as in Gson, where the fields are final and a Gson is
     never changed after it is built; here the copy is what keeps that promise
     (see GsonBuilder.create). */
  void setPrettyPrinting(boolean v) {
    prettyPrinting = v
  }

  void setHtmlSafe(boolean v) {
    htmlSafe = v
  }

  void setSerializeNulls(boolean v) {
    serializeNulls = v
  }

  void setLenient(boolean v) {
    lenient = v
  }

  void setExcludeModifiers(int v) {
    excludeModifiers = v
  }

  void setExposeOnly(boolean v) {
    exposeOnly = v
  }

  void setNaming(FieldNamingPolicy v) {
    naming = v
  }

  void setVersion(double v) {
    version = v
  }

  void register(Class type, Object adapter, boolean hierarchy) {
    adapters.add(type, adapter, hierarchy)
  }

  // ---- writing ----------------------------------------------------------

  public String toJson(JsonElement jsonElement) {
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder()
    toJson(jsonElement, sb)
    return sb.toString()
  }

  /* Gson's toJson(Object, Appendable): the document is written into the sink as
     it is produced. The sink here is a StringBuilder, which is the appendable
     this standard library has -- there is no java.lang.Appendable and no
     java.io.Writer (see lib/03_io.teyru) -- and a document that should not be
     built in memory at all is written with a JsonWriter instead (JsonWriter
     takes the sink directly). */
  public void toJson(Object value, StringBuilder out) {
    JsonWriter writer = newJsonWriter(out)
    JsonWriter.write(value == null ? JsonNull.INSTANCE : JsonBinding.writeTo(value, this), writer)
  }

  public void toJson(JsonElement element, StringBuilder out) {
    JsonWriter writer = newJsonWriter(out)
    JsonWriter.write(element, writer)
  }

  // Gson's newJsonWriter: a writer configured the way this Gson writes.
  public JsonWriter newJsonWriter(StringBuilder out) {
    JsonWriter writer = new JsonWriter(out)
    if (prettyPrinting) {
      // Gson's setPrettyPrinting is setIndent("  ")
      writer.setIndent("  ")
    }
    writer.setHtmlSafe(htmlSafe)
    writer.setSerializeNulls(serializeNulls)
    return writer
  }

  /* Gson's toJson(Object): the value's own class answers, so a call whose static
     type says nothing about the value still writes what it is. */
  public String toJson(Object value) {
    return toJson(JsonBinding.writeTo(value, this))
  }

  // Gson's toJsonTree: the tree the binding would write, before it is text.
  public JsonElement toJsonTree(Object value) {
    return value == null ? JsonNull.INSTANCE : JsonBinding.writeTo(value, this)
  }

  // ---- reading ---------------------------------------------------------

  /* Gson's fromJson(json, JsonElement.class). The Class-taking overloads are
     absent because a class literal has type Object here (see the header), so
     the cast at the call site -- (JsonObject) gson.fromJson(text) -- stands in
     for the typed overload. */
  public JsonElement fromJson(String json) {
    if (json == null || json.length() == 0) {
      return null
    }
    JsonReader reader = newJsonReader(json)
    return reader.parseDocument()
  }

  // Gson's newJsonReader: a reader configured the way this Gson reads.
  public JsonReader newJsonReader(String json) {
    JsonReader reader = new JsonReader(json)
    reader.setLenient(lenient)
    return reader
  }

  /* Gson's fromJson(json, Class): the class's fields are read off the class
     itself, which is what Gson's reflection does and what this binding does now.
     The type parameter is Gson's own spelling -- `Person p = gson.fromJson(s,
     Person.class)` -- and the cast it saves the caller is the one the compiler
     writes here. */
  public <T> T fromJson(String json, Class type) {
    if (json == null || json.length() == 0) {
      return null
    }
    // the witness is written out: this language infers less than javac does, and
    // a value returned as the method's own type parameter is not enough to
    // resolve readFrom's
    return JsonBinding.<T>readFrom(json, type, this)
  }

  /* Gson's fromJson(JsonElement, Class), for a document that has already been
     read -- from a tree an adapter was handed, or one built by hand. */
  public <T> T fromJson(JsonElement json, Class type) {
    if (json == null || json.isJsonNull()) {
      return null
    }
    return JsonBinding.<T>read(json, type, this)
  }

  // ---- builder ---------------------------------------------------------

  /* Gson's newBuilder and toBuilder: a builder holding a copy of this
     configuration, so that what is built from it is this Gson plus the
     difference. */
  public GsonBuilder newBuilder() {
    return new GsonBuilder(new Gson(this))
  }

  public GsonBuilder toBuilder() {
    return newBuilder()
  }
}

/* com.google.gson.GsonBuilder. Each setter records one thing about the
   configuration and answers the builder, so the calls chain the way Gson's do.

   The configuration it writes on is the builder's own Gson, and create() hands
   out a copy of it: a Gson that has been created is never changed again, which
   is what lets JsonBinding cache a reader and a writer against it. */
class GsonBuilder {
  private Gson config

  public GsonBuilder() {
    config = new Gson()
  }

  GsonBuilder(Gson start) {
    config = start
  }

  public GsonBuilder setPrettyPrinting() {
    config.setPrettyPrinting(true)
    return this
  }

  public GsonBuilder disableHtmlEscaping() {
    config.setHtmlSafe(false)
    return this
  }

  public GsonBuilder serializeNulls() {
    config.setSerializeNulls(true)
    return this
  }

  /* Gson's setLenient: the documents this Gson reads may be as loose as Gson's
     own reader allows -- unquoted names, single-quoted strings, a leading +, a
     leading '.', a trailing ',', NaN and Infinity. It is false by default here,
     where the rest of the reader is strict (see the class comment above). */
  public GsonBuilder setLenient(boolean lenient) {
    config.setLenient(lenient)
    return this
  }

  /* Gson's excludeFieldsWithModifiers: a field whose modifiers overlap the set
     is not bound. It REPLACES the default set, as it does in Gson -- a field
     that is static or transient is then only excluded when the call names it,
     so `excludeFieldsWithModifiers()` with no arguments binds everything that
     is not synthetic. */
  public GsonBuilder excludeFieldsWithModifiers(int... modifiers) {
    int mask = 0
    for (int i = 0 : i < modifiers.length : i++) {
      mask = mask | modifiers[i]
    }
    config.setExcludeModifiers(mask)
    return this
  }

  // Gson's excludeFieldsWithoutExposeAnnotation.
  public GsonBuilder excludeFieldsWithoutExposeAnnotation() {
    config.setExposeOnly(true)
    return this
  }

  public GsonBuilder setFieldNamingPolicy(FieldNamingPolicy namingPolicy) {
    config.setNaming(namingPolicy)
    return this
  }

  /* Gson's setVersion: the version @Since and @Until are read against. A field
     is skipped when the version is below its @Since, or at or above its
     @Until. */
  public GsonBuilder setVersion(double version) {
    config.setVersion(version)
    return this
  }

  /* Gson's registerTypeAdapter: the adapter answers for that type and no other.
     The object must implement JsonSerializer, JsonDeserializer, or both; the
     last registration for a type wins, as in Gson. */
  public GsonBuilder registerTypeAdapter(Class type, Object adapter) {
    checkAdapter(type, adapter)
    config.register(type, adapter, false)
    return this
  }

  /* Gson's registerTypeHierarchyAdapter: the adapter answers for that type and
     every subtype of it. */
  public GsonBuilder registerTypeHierarchyAdapter(Class baseType, Object adapter) {
    checkAdapter(baseType, adapter)
    config.register(baseType, adapter, true)
    return this
  }

  // checkAdapter is Gson's up-front refusal of an adapter that does nothing,
  // raised where the registration is written rather than at the first value.
  private void checkAdapter(Class type, Object adapter) {
    if (adapter == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("adapter must not be null")
    }
    if (!(adapter instanceof JsonSerializer) && !(adapter instanceof JsonDeserializer)) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException(adapter.getClass().getName() +
        " is not a JsonSerializer or a JsonDeserializer")
    }
    if (type == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("type must not be null")
    }
  }

  /* Gson's create: a copy of the configuration, so that changing the builder
     afterwards cannot change a Gson that was already handed out. */
  public Gson create() {
    return new Gson(config)
  }
}
